add('structure','10b6–9 concludes the year-unit derivations after the planting reconciliation.10b10–11a2 gives two attributed chronology lists; subsequent segments derive individual entries. Core ends during Isaac’s gestation answer; read the remaining chronology/flood continuations through12a5, with12a6a new heading.','10b:6','10b:9','10b:10','11a:2','11a:15','12a:5','12a:6')
add('transmission','Rabbi Yohanan says both year-counting authorities derive their views from Genesis8:13. Retain this report as distinct from the individual interpretations.','10b:6')
add('derivation','Rabbi Meir infers from one elapsed day being called a year that one day in a year counts as a year.','10b:6')
add('counterreading','The other authority says שנה modifies the600years, whereas אחת refers to the beginning of the next year; the alternative syntax would have supported Meir, but is not the printed wording.','10b:7')
add('derivation','The abbreviated ר״א derives one day counting as a month from בראשון באחד לחדש. Preserve abbreviation and the prior printed אלעזר/אליעזר distinction rather than silently identifying this authority with the later creation speaker.','10b:8')
add('derivation','From one day counting as a month, thirty days count as a year; month and year each follow their constituent counting units. This is the thirty-day authority’s reasoning.','10b:8')
add('editorial_inference','The parenthetical מכלל infers that both accept creation in Nisan. Retain parentheses and inference status; it is not direct named speech.','10b:9')
# Atomic chronology positions, each tied to explicit speaker list.
for holder,ref,items in [('Rabbi Eliezer','10b:10',[('creation','Creation occurred in Tishrei.'),('patriarch_birth','The patriarchs were born in Tishrei.'),('patriarch_death','The patriarchs died in Tishrei.'),('isaac','Isaac was born at Passover.'),('sarah','Sarah was remembered on Rosh Hashanah.'),('rachel','Rachel was remembered on Rosh Hashanah.'),('hannah','Hannah was remembered on Rosh Hashanah.'),('joseph','Joseph left prison on Rosh Hashanah.')]),('Rabbi Eliezer continued','11a:1',[('labor','Labor ceased for the ancestors in Egypt on Rosh Hashanah.'),('past_redemption','They were redeemed in Nisan.'),('future_redemption','They will be redeemed in Tishrei.')]),('Rabbi Yehoshua','11a:2',[('creation','Creation occurred in Nisan.'),('patriarch_birth','The patriarchs were born in Nisan.'),('patriarch_death','The patriarchs died in Nisan.'),('isaac','Isaac was born at Passover.'),('sarah','Sarah was remembered on Rosh Hashanah.'),('rachel','Rachel was remembered on Rosh Hashanah.'),('hannah','Hannah was remembered on Rosh Hashanah.'),('joseph','Joseph left prison on Rosh Hashanah.'),('labor','Labor ceased for the ancestors in Egypt on Rosh Hashanah.'),('past_redemption','They were redeemed in Nisan.'),('future_redemption','They will be redeemed in Nisan.')])]:
 for label,t in items:add('attributed_chronology',holder+' states: '+t+' Preserve this as the baraita’s attributed teaching, not an independently established historical fact.',ref)
add('support','Rabbi Eliezer supports Tishrei creation from Genesis1:11 grass-sprouting and fruit-laden trees; the question identifies the corresponding month as Tishrei.','11a:3')
add('support','That account places rain at the first rainfall season and cites the mist rising from the earth. Keep the supplementary rainfall support distinct from the vegetation argument.','11a:3')
add('support','Rabbi Yehoshua supports Nisan creation from Genesis1:12 earth filled with grass and trees producing fruit.','11a:4')
add('support','That account also cites mating season for animals and birds and לבשו כרים הצאן. This supports the Nisan account, not a separate dispute about mating.','11a:4')
add('objection_answer','עץ עושה פרי is raised against the opposing Tishrei account; answer: it expresses a blessing for later generations. This answers the verse conflict rather than retracting Tishrei creation.','11a:5')
add('objection_answer','עץ פרי is raised against the Nisan account; answer invokes Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi’s creation teaching. Do not conflate him with Rabbi Yehoshua.','11a:6')
add('quoted_claim','Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says creation’s works were created at their stature; the raw לקומתן is parenthesized. Preserve this textual feature.','11a:6')
add('quoted_claim','Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says creation’s works were created with their knowledge/consent (לדעתן). Keep this separate from stature and desired form.','11a:6')
add('quoted_claim','Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says creation’s works were created according to their desired form (לצביונן). His אל תקרי reads צבאם as צביונם. Retain the interpretive wordplay rather than altering the source verse.','11a:6')
add('derivation','Rabbi Eliezer derives Tishrei patriarchal births from ירח האיתנים, interpreting it as the month the mighty ones of the world were born.','11a:7')
add('question_support','The Gemara asks how איתן means mighty and cites איתן מושבך and האיתנים מוסדי ארץ. Preserve the lexical question and its scriptural supports.','11a:8')
add('derivation','The hills/mountains verse is interpreted: leaping over mountains through the merit of patriarchs, over hills through the merit of matriarchs. Preserve both distinct subjects and merit claims.','11a:8')
add('derivation','Rabbi Yehoshua derives Nisan patriarchal births from חודש זיו as the month in which the radiant ones of the world were born. Keep quoted verse wording without silently correcting its stated month.','11a:9')
add('objection_answer','ירח האיתנים challenges the other account; answer interprets it as strong in commandments. It is an alternative interpretation of the same epithet.','11a:10')
add('objection_answer','חדש זיו challenges the other account; answer interprets it as radiance of the trees. This is supported by Rav Yehuda’s Nisan blossoming blessing.','11a:11')
add('quoted_rule','Rav Yehuda says one who goes out in Nisan and sees blossoming trees recites the printed blessing thanking God for lacking nothing and creating good beings/trees for human enjoyment. Retain exact blessing quotation and observation/month conditions; do not silently replace it with a contemporary liturgical version.','11a:11')
add('conditional_chronology','For the view that the patriarchs were born in Nisan, they died in Nisan; for the Tishrei-birth view, they died in Tishrei. This preserves paired alternatives rather than assigning only one death date.','11a:12')
add('derivation','Moses’s היום is treated as indicating completion of his days and years, teaching that God completes the righteous persons’ years day-to-day and month-to-month, with את מספר ימיך אמלא as support. The argument supplies both paired death-month claims.','11a:12')
add('question','What establishes Isaac’s Passover birth? The verse למועד אשוב אליך starts a sequence of possible announcement dates.','11a:13')
add('rejected_case','If the announcement were Passover for Shavuot, fifty days would be insufficient for childbirth; that candidate is rejected.','11a:13')
add('rejected_case','If the announcement were Shavuot for Tishrei, five months would be insufficient; that candidate is rejected.','11a:13')
add('accepted_case','The answer locates the announcement at the festival in Tishrei and the promised return in Nisan. This is then tested against duration objections, not simply asserted without conditions.','11a:13')
add('objection_answer','Six months still seems insufficient; a cited teaching answers that the year was intercalated. Preserve the leap-year condition.','11a:14')
add('further_objection','After subtracting days of impurity, the period still seems short. This question motivates Mar Zutra’s next distinction.','11a:14')
add('attributed_distinction','Mar Zutra says even according to the view that a nine-month pregnancy cannot deliver in a partial period, a seven-month pregnancy can deliver in a partial period. This is the text’s explanatory claim, not modern medical guidance or an unqualified rule about all births.','11a:15')
add('derivation','Mar Zutra cites לתקופות הימים: minimum תקופות is two and minimum ימים two. Preserve the minimal-unit calculation and its role in the partial-period answer.','11a:15')
add('context_derivation','Rabbi Elazar (printed אלעזר) derives the Rosh Hashanah remembrance of Sarah, Rachel and Hannah through זכירה/פקידה analogies; Rachel and Hannah remembrance connect to זכרון תרועה. Do not conflate this named Elazar with Eliezer of the creation list.','11a:16')
add('context_derivation','The פקידה link connects Hannah to Sarah via כי פקד and וה׳ פקד. This completes the chain begun in the prior segment.','11a:17')
add('context_derivation','Joseph’s Rosh Hashanah release is linked through Psalm81 shofar/festival wording to עדות ביהוסף...בצאתו, crossing11a18–11b1. Do not separate the proof at the folio boundary.','11a:18','11b:1')
add('context_derivation','The ancestors’ cessation of labor on Rosh Hashanah uses the shared burden language סבלות/מסבל connecting Exodus and the Psalm.','11b:2')
add('context_derivation','For the Tishrei future redemption account, שופר–שופר connects the festival shofar to Isaiah’s great shofar. Past Nisan redemption is treated as already established.','11b:3')
add('context_derivation','Rabbi Yehoshua supports future Nisan redemption from ליל שמורים, a night reserved from creation.','11b:4')
add('context_counterreading','The other account reads ליל שמורים as protected from harmful forces; retain it as an alternative verse allocation.','11b:5')
add('context_position','The flood discussion connects to the preceding views: Rabbi Yehoshua identifies the day as17Iyar, when Kimah ordinarily sets by day and springs decrease, with altered conduct answered by altered creation, two stars removed and flood brought. Preserve attributed cosmology, not modern astronomy.','11b:6')
add('context_position','Rabbi Eliezer identifies17Marheshvan, with Kimah rising by day and increasing springs. The continuation also describes altered creation and removal of two stars; preserve the apparent overlap rather than silently repairing the text.','11b:7','12a:1')
add('context_question_answer','שני fits Rabbi Yehoshua’s month account; for Rabbi Eliezer it is answered as second to judgment.','12a:2')
add('context_question_answer','The question what changed under Rabbi Eliezer’s account is answered through Rav Hisda: heated misconduct was punished with heated water, supported by וישכו/שככה. Keep attribution and interpretive analogy.','12a:3','12a:4')
add('context_classification','The cited baraita says Israel’s sages count the flood according to Rabbi Eliezer but the seasonal cycle according to Rabbi Yehoshua; sages of the nations count even the flood according to Rabbi Yehoshua. Distinguish the two calculation domains and groups.','12a:5')
add('boundary','12a6 begins the vegetables/tithes/vows heading. It ends the relevant chronology/flood context and is not a new covered core here.','12a:6')
